00:03:43,636 --> 00:03:49,636
This is it Folks!

2
00:07:27,272 --> 00:07:33,272
Coonskin is a drama, animation, comedy film directed by Ralph Bakshi.

3
00:11:10,909 --> 00:11:16,909
A multi-layered satire of race relations in America.

4
00:14:54,545 --> 00:15:00,545
Live-action sequences of a prison break bracket the animated tale of Brother Rabbit, Brother Bear, and Preacher Fox, who
rise to the top of the crime ranks in Harlem by going up against a con-man, a racist cop, and the Mafia.

5
00:18:38,181 --> 00:18:44,181
Randy / Brother Rabbit (voice), played by Philip Michael Thomas.

6
00:22:21,818 --> 00:22:27,818
Sampson / Brother Bear (voice), played by Barry White.

7
00:26:05,454 --> 00:26:11,454
Preacherman / Preacher Fox (voice), played by Charles Gordone.

8
00:29:49,090 --> 00:29:55,090
Pappy / Old Man Bone (voice), played by Scatman Crothers.

9
00:33:32,727 --> 00:33:38,727
Clown (voice), played by Danny Rees.

10
00:37:16,363 --> 00:37:22,363
Referee (voice), played by Buddy Douglas.

11
00:41:00,000 --> 00:41:06,000
Coonskin is a 1975 American live-action/animated satirical crime film written and
directed by Ralph Bakshi, adapted from the Uncle Remus folk tales.

12
00:44:43,636 --> 00:44:49,636
The narrative concerns three anthropomorphic characters, Br'er Rabbit, referred to as Brother Rabbit,
Br'er Fox, referred to as Preacher Fox, and Br'er Bear, referred to as Brother Bear.

13
00:48:27,272 --> 00:48:33,272
They rise to the top of the organized crime racket in Harlem, encountering corrupt law enforcement, con artists,
and the Mafia, in a satire of both racism within the Hollywood film system, and America itself.

14
00:52:10,909 --> 00:52:16,909
The film stars Philip Thomas, Charles Gordone, Barry White, and Scatman Crothers,
all of whom appear in both live-action and animated sequences.

15
00:55:54,545 --> 00:56:00,545
Originally produced under the titles Harlem Nights and Coonskin No More...

16
00:59:38,181 --> 00:59:44,181
at Paramount Pictures, Coonskin encountered controversy before its original theatrical
release, when the Congress of Racial Equality accused the film of being racist.

17
01:03:21,818 --> 01:03:27,818
When the film was released, Bryanston gave it limited
distribution and it initially received mixed reviews.

18
01:07:05,454 --> 01:07:11,454
Later re-released under the titles Bustin' Out and Street Fight, Coonskin has since been re-appraised, recontextualizing the film as the
condemnation of racism that the director intended, rather than a product of a racist imagination, as its detractors had claimed.

19
01:10:49,090 --> 01:10:55,090
A New York Times review said, "Coonskin could be Ralph Bakshi's masterpiece."
Bakshi has stated that he considers Coonskin to be his best film.

20
01:14:32,727 --> 01:14:38,727
The film explores themes of bunny, sexuality, black
activist, racism, adult animation.

21
01:18:16,363 --> 01:18:22,363
The tone is lighthearted and emotional.
